Multigrep 

By: 
Quan Pham 
Judas Smith 

This development project is a multithreaded implementation of grep, in which heuristics are used to determine the optimal number of threads to navigate a particular filepath quickly and efficiently. The project focuses on proper multithreading practices, ensuring that the utility works seamlessly with all other existing UNIX tools as though it was the original grep. Users can specify much of the behavior through additional flags, including the search method (breadth vs depth first), output order (process 0 followed by process 1... vs interleaved), and more. The project should take approximately 4k lines of code. 

 

Report 1: 

During this increment we decided exactly which search behaviors would be modifiable. Users will be able to choose from the following list, with the potential for these choices to be made at the program’s discretion via heuristics if time allows. Many of these features are present in grep, and so must be implemented, but others are unique to this program. 

Modifiable Behaviors: 
Full or partial word search (If looking for “the” do we include “mother”). 
Capitalization match (If looking for “who” do we include “WHO”). 
End of line marker (Typically “\n”, but could be any character). 
Ignored characters (If looking for “my” and ignoring “.” do we include “m.y”). 
Fuzz level (If looking for “cool” do we include “coop” “pool” or “c_ool”). 
Work distribution (Does each process get a line of text? A file? A single portion of a larger pattern?). 
Output order (Does each process output as it finds them, or does process 0 output followed by 1, etc…). 
Search method (Breadth first or depth first).  

Much of the increment was spent researching the topic, with some of the most impactful insights coming from “agrep”, an open-source grep alternative first published in 1988. Through agrep we were able to better understand the internal workings of grep by extension and were given significantly more direction for how best to structure our implementation. Credit must also be given to GNU’s documentation for grep 3.11, as it gave us an out from having to painstakingly walk through the “man” pages. 

