Report 2: 

During this increment, we implemented a sequential version of grep based on GNU’s 3.11 grep documentation and the opensource versions of grep previously detailed.  

Of the behaviors listed in the last report, some have been changed. An updated list with each’s anticipated solution is below. 

 

Modifiable Behaviors: 

Full or partial word search (If looking for “the” do we include “mother”)? 
Grep has special “anchoring” characters, ^ and $ for preceding and following whitespace, respectively. This is likely to be reviewed and changed, but for now adding extra spaces in the search term suffices. 

Capitalization match (If looking for “who” do we include “WHO”). 
This has been implemented as an additional flag, “-c” for ignore capitalization. All characters are treated as lowercase when searching for a matching pattern. 

End of line marker (Typically “\n”, but could be any character). 
This has been implemented as the flag “-t” for terminator. It allows the user to specify their own terminating character. 

Ignored characters (If looking for “my” and ignoring “.” do we include “m.y”). 
This has been implemented as the flag “-i". It allows the user to specify characters to be removed from a particular line before searching for a pattern match. 

Fuzz level (If looking for “cool” do we include “coop” “pool” or “c_ool”). 
This has proven more complicated than anticipated and requires more consideration before being implemented. Our working idea is as follows: If the fuzz level is 1, when searching for “cool” “coop” or “pool” would be allowed. “c_ool” would be a fuzz level of 2, as there are two characters that do not match. 

Work distribution: Each Worker will work on a portion of text. We will use box allocation for this project to minimize the amount of communication between workers. 

Output order (Does each worker output as it finds them, or does worker 0 output followed by 1, etc…). 
Search method (Breadth first or depth first). 
The remaining behaviors will be better specified as progress is made on parallelization. 

 

As of now, we have decided our best option for parallelization is to use openMP and potentially MPI. Once fully functional, we may rework into pthreads, though much of what we would change with pthreads can also be managed through more detailed openMP pragma use. 